<content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<para>Here is a graph of the accumulated rainfall in Las Vegas, Nevada, in the first 60 days of 2017.</para>
<media><image alt="CONTINUOUS GRAPH WITH TITLE OF ACCUMULATED RAINFALL, LAS VEGAS, NEVADA, X-AXIS LABEL OF DAY OF THE YEAR 2017, AND Y-AXIS LABEL OF RAINFALL IN INCHES. THE X-AXIS GOES FROM 0 TO 60 IN INCREMENTS OF 10. THE Y-AXIS GOES FROM 0 TO 1-POINT-6 IN INCREMENTS OF 0-POINT-2." src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7e30c2db1551fdc9b632b0f38c4f8ca6b85d803d"/></media><para>Use the graph to support your answers to the following questions.</para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Is the accumulated amount of rainfall a function of time? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Yes. For each number of days \(d\) between 0 and 60, a certain amount of rain fell up to that time, so there is one particular value for the accumulated rain at any point in time.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Is time a function of accumulated rainfall? </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>No. There was no rainfall in the first 10 days. This means that for the amount of rainfall 0 inches, or for the input value of 0, there are lots of times \(d\) that could be the output.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
